WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.969
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Tom Hanks.

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.271
So we were talking about your
son Truman making his debut.

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:08.541
-Now, he got -- Marc Forster
wanted him because we resemble

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:11.477
each other almost exactly
from the age of 26.

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:13.613
-Yes.
But you -- This is not him.

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:15.581
This is you probably
around the age of 26.

00:00:15.581 --> 00:00:18.685
This is your first film,
"He Knows You're Alone."

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:21.687
-Do you remember anything about
1980s, "He Knows You're Alone"?

00:00:21.687 --> 00:00:27.193
-That was -- I played --
There was a -- There was a --

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:30.430
There was a psychotic killer
that was killing women

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.966
who were about to get married,
would-be brides.

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:35.201
-Uh-huh.
-And, by the way, that's my

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.503
own jacket,
and that's my own wardrobe.

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:38.437
[ Laughter ]

00:00:38.437 --> 00:00:41.674
-It was January 1980
or something like that.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:44.210
And somehow I got the part.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.413
And I had to get up at 5:30 in
the morning and meet somebody

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:51.183
at 5:45 on the corner of, like,
9th Avenue and 43rd

00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:54.253
to drive off to Staten Island
where we were shooting it.

00:00:54.253 --> 00:00:58.591
And I vomited the morning
I got up, I was so nervous.

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:00.926
-Wow.
-I upchucked. I knew my --

00:01:00.926 --> 00:01:03.296
I knew my dialogue,
but just the whole concept

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:05.131
of being in a movie
was terrifying.

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:07.566
-Now, when the agent called and
told you you have the part,

00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:09.269
did they say,
"bring your own jacket"?

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:10.103
-Uh...

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:11.137
[ Laughter ]

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.471
You know, it was like --

00:01:12.471 --> 00:01:16.275
The movie cost like $400,000
or something like that.

00:01:16.275 --> 00:01:17.977
They just said, "somebody will
call you,"

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:19.311
and thank God somebody did.

00:01:19.311 --> 00:01:22.582
-I think this is the first time
I saw you on-screen,

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.750
was this film
"Mazes and Monsters."

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.886
-Oh, my God.
-Now, "Mazes and Monsters,"

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:30.389
by the poster, you think this is
a super cool sort of,

00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:32.425
you know, "Lord of
the Rings"-type film.

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.193
[ Laughter ]
That is not the case.

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:35.795
-No. By the way, they gave me

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:37.496
some damn chiseled good looks
there.

00:01:37.496 --> 00:01:39.299
-Yeah, they really --
-That's something else.

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:41.600
By the way, this was not
the billing when we made it.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.069
-No, absolutely.
-It was on CBS.

00:01:44.069 --> 00:01:45.505
Steven Stern directed.

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:47.473
Oh, yeah, Wendy Crewson.
She's fabulous.

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:49.208
She was in
"From the Earth to the Moon."

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:51.811
David Wallace. Lloyd Bochner,
who was a big deal.

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:53.579
Peter Donat. Chris Makepeace.

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:55.414
-Chris Makepeace is
in "My Bodyguard."

00:01:55.414 --> 00:01:57.149
-"My Bodyguard," yeah.

00:01:57.149 --> 00:02:00.019
It was about mazes -- kids
playing "Mazes and Monsters."

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:00.954
-Which was a Dungeons &amp; Dra--
Yeah.

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:02.655
-No, no, Dungeons &amp; Dragons.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:07.059
-And you played a fella who got
so deep into the world

00:02:07.059 --> 00:02:08.228
of Dungeons &amp; Dragons --

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:10.863
-I began to see mazes
and monsters everywhere.

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:13.399
And I fled to New York City
and had a scene

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:14.733
on the top of
the World Trade Center.

00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:17.537
-Really? This is a film that as
I watched as a kid,

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:20.072
it felt like a cautionary tale
to parents,

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:22.775
which was like, "Don't let your
kids play Dungeons &amp; Dragons."

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:24.510
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.744
-And did you?

00:02:25.744 --> 00:02:27.480
-No, I didn't,
and here I still am.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.449
-And here you are,
so let's -- let's --

00:02:30.449 --> 00:02:33.986
We did the -- It had all
the gibberishy kind of names.

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:36.689
Beware of the Castle of
Thangoria.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:39.926
And you shall go to
the Towers of Pabla--

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:41.527
You know, it's a little tough.

00:02:41.527 --> 00:02:44.263
-Made it a little bit --
-But, look, I had a job.

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:47.166
I was the most thrilled guy
there was.

00:02:47.166 --> 00:02:51.504
I was actually, I'm going to
say, 24 when I made that.

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:54.607
-Next time you're here, do I
have permission to introduce you

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:56.408
saying "you know him from
'Mazes and Monsters'"?

00:02:56.408 --> 00:02:57.710
-I think.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.379
I think that is going to be
a requirement, I believe.

00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:02.948
-You have a wonderful co-star
in this film,

00:03:02.948 --> 00:03:05.151
which is this lovely --
I have a photo.

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:07.587
You have a lovely cat.
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:11.824
-Obviously you've worked with
dogs, children, volleyballs.

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.092
-Yes.
-How was --

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:15.361
[ Laughter ]
How was a cat co-star?

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.263
Same cat the whole movie?
-Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:19.264
Smeagol. Smeagol Bagel.

00:03:19.264 --> 00:03:22.201
You cannot train a cat to do
everything -- anything.

00:03:22.201 --> 00:03:25.805
They are the most disinterested
creatures on the planet Earth.

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:29.441
Dogs will do exactly
what they are trained to do,

00:03:29.441 --> 00:03:32.010
including look at you adoringly,
right?

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.280
Because you work with a dog
and the trainer says --

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.449
They say the word "bond,"
you know?

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:39.285
So the dog only looks at
the trainer all the time.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:41.854
Just say "bond."
-Bond.

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:45.958
[ Laughter ]

00:03:45.958 --> 00:03:47.794
-Then they come back.

00:03:47.794 --> 00:03:49.995
That's literally what
they will do.

00:03:49.995 --> 00:03:51.797
And you can even say
"bond sadder."

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:54.066
Say "bond sadder."
-Bond sadder.

00:03:54.066 --> 00:03:56.736
Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:58.003
-And then they will come back.

00:03:58.003 --> 00:03:59.505
They have no idea
what they're doing.

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:02.975
But cats, cats are untrainable.

00:04:02.975 --> 00:04:06.012
Cats lay around like this,
you know.

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:08.414
During the shot, the cat is
supposed to be in the bed.

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:09.982
The cat is supposed to be
sleeping.

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:11.784
The cat's like this.

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:14.954
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:17.990
And this had to roll and roll
and roll because they wanted

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:19.759
a moment where the cat
looked at Otto.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.494
And the cat eventually did this.

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:25.498
[ Laughter ]

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:27.333
And that was it.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:30.869
-Do you ever have to sort of
have that awful moment

00:04:30.869 --> 00:04:32.972
where you're, like,
waving something,

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:34.073
trying to get
the cat's attention?

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:35.641
-Oh, off-camera?
-Yeah.

00:04:35.641 --> 00:04:37.977
Smeagol, Smeagol.
[ Clicking tongue ]

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:40.113
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:42.215
Nothing. Nothing.
-Yeah.

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:43.682
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:46.152
But, now, is this true that
the cat was at the premiere?

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:47.420
-Yeah, we brought him along.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.254
And he let me hold him again.

00:04:49.254 --> 00:04:50.890
That was the big thing.
-Wow.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.524
-Because literally that shot,

00:04:52.524 --> 00:04:54.627
I had to hold him
for a couple hours.

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:57.997
And the cat has to decide
that you can hold him.

00:04:57.997 --> 00:04:59.265
-Yeah.

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:02.068
-And you had to do it in
a very specific kind of way.

00:05:02.068 --> 00:05:04.737
And, you know, you do
that kind of stuff.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:07.140
And I was coached
for a long time.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:11.844
And also the two trainers were
right there right off of camera.

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:15.214
And, yes, it's true no cat
was forced to do

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:17.049
any damn thing
it didn't want to

00:05:17.049 --> 00:05:19.752
in the course of making
this motion picture.

00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:23.122
-I would expect
nothing less of you, Tom Hanks.

00:05:23.122 --> 00:05:25.358
-Held sway.
-Congratulations on the film.

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:26.659
It's always so lovely to have
you here. Thank you so much.

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:28.393
-Thank you. Thank you very much.

00:05:28.393 --> 00:05:30.629
-Tom Hanks, everybody.

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:32.665
"A Man Called Otto"
is in theaters

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:36.035
everywhere on January 13th.

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:38.738
We'll be right back
with Jessie Buckley.
-Big winner.

